 ## Overview
-Projection and MIP Techniques are a family of volume rendering methods that generate 2D images by aggregating scalar values along viewing rays without using the standard alpha-blending optical model (emission/absorption). Unlike standard ray casting which composites translucent surfaces, these methods highlight specific intensity characteristics—such as the maximum, minimum, or total sum—making them order-independent and ideal for visualizing high-contrast structures like bones or vessels [S1, unofficial][S2][S3, unofficial][S4]. They differ from siblings like [Compositing and Blending](2515-compositing-and-blending.md) by discarding depth cues and opacity accumulation in favor of scalar extrema or integrals.
+Projection and MIP Techniques are a family of volume rendering methods that generate 2D images by aggregating scalar values along viewing rays without using the standard alpha-blending optical model (emission/absorption) [G]. They differ from siblings like [Compositing and Blending](2515-compositing-and-blending.md) by discarding depth cues and opacity accumulation in favor of scalar extrema or integrals [G]. MIP was introduced as a standard technique in medical imaging visualization to overcome the limitations of alpha blending in highlighting high-contrast structures [G]. The technique is widely supported in medical imaging workstations and is a fundamental tool for angiography and bone visualization [G].
 
